<p>Graphs are finite unless stated otherwise. A simple graph has no loops o=
r parallel edges. Directed graphs can have loops when explicitly allowed. A=
 walk can repeat vertices; a path cannot. Path length counts edges. Reachab=
ility includes length-zero walks unless a strict reachability relation is e=
xplicitly requested. Probability examples use discrete spaces; countably in=
finite sums are handled with convergence conditions stated where needed.</p=
>
<p>The source sometimes uses terminology differently. In particular, strict=
 and weak partial orders are distinguished explicitly here; graph-network m=
easurements state whether input/output terminal edges count. The source's m=
inor characterization of planarity is conventionally called Wagner's theore=
m; Kuratowski's theorem uses subdivisions. Both appear with that distinctio=
n in Chapter 11.</p>
<p>This book teaches mathematical foundations for CS. It is not a complete =
degree in mathematics, and it does not add full courses in calculus, linear=
 algebra, statistics, or numerical analysis that the PDF does not contain. =
Those are sensible subsequent studies once these discrete foundations are s=
ecure.</p>
