<p><a id=3D"chapter-01-section-10"></a></p>
<h3>Rates, proportions, and units</h3>
<p>A rate connects two kinds of quantity. If a process handles eight record=
s per second, then processing twenty-four records takes three seconds under=
 a constant-rate model. Dividing records by records per second leaves secon=
ds. This dimensional check is useful even in abstract CS examples: bytes, b=
its, comparisons, seconds, and probabilities should not be silently mixed.<=
/p>
<p>A percentage is a fraction with denominator one hundred. Twenty percent =
means one fifth. Increasing a quantity by twenty percent multiplies it by 1=
.2; decreasing the result by twenty percent multiplies it by 0.8. Their pro=
duct is 0.96, so the two changes do not cancel. A rise from probability 0.1=
 to 0.2 is an increase of 0.1, or ten percentage points, and a relative inc=
rease of one hundred percent. Both descriptions are correct but answer diff=
erent questions.</p>
<p>This matters when comparing algorithms. A runtime falling from ten secon=
ds to five has decreased by fifty percent, while the processing rate has do=
ubled. Before accepting a numerical improvement claim, identify which quant=
ity is being compared and what denominator defines the percentage. Mathemat=
ics cannot repair an ambiguous measurement after the fact; the model must s=
pecify it.</p>
