<p><a id=3D"chapter-01-section-13"></a></p>
<h3>Working habits and optional practice</h3>
<p>Use a line for each substantial algebraic operation. Keep restrictions v=
isible, especially denominators, square roots, and integer constraints. Che=
ck a proposed identity at an easy value to catch mistakes, while rememberin=
g that a successful check is not a proof. Draw a number line for inequaliti=
es and list a tiny instance before writing a general sum. These are ways to=
 discover reasoning; the later proof must explain why the reasoning applies=
 to all permitted inputs.</p>
