<p>Why count a false-premise implication as true? A universal rule about ad=
ministrators is violated by an administrator denied access; it says nothing=
 about what happens to a nonadministrator. An input outside the rule's hypo=
thesis cannot refute the rule. This is called vacuous truth. It is a conven=
tion with useful and consistent consequences, not a claim that false assump=
tions reveal facts about the world.</p>
