<p><a id=3D"chapter-03"></a></p>
<h2>03. Building and checking proofs</h2>
<p>Suppose a program works on a million inputs. Why might it fail on anothe=
r? A test gives information about the cases actually tested. A theorem give=
s a conditional guarantee about every case described by its hypotheses. The=
 bridge is a proof: an argument using definitions, accepted assumptions, an=
d valid deduction. This chapter develops proof patterns without treating th=
em as incantations.</p>
<p>The source is Chapter 1, with logic from Chapter 02. We accept ordinary =
arithmetic laws as background. An axiom is an assumption accepted within a =
theory; a theorem is established from assumptions. A lemma is a useful supp=
orting theorem, and a corollary follows quickly from another result. These =
labels describe roles rather than different degrees of truth.</p>
