<p>The final presentation should identify each dependency. We used the defi=
nition of oddness, distributivity, the parity of consecutive integers, and =
the definition of a remainder. We did not use the result for the first few =
odd numbers as an inference rule. Trying three or five was helpful for disc=
overing the pattern but not the reason it continues.</p>
<p>Reasoning backward is especially useful with existential targets. To fin=
d integers satisfying a linear equation, first ask what combination would p=
roduce the desired remainder, then use an established construction such as =
extended Euclid. When the goal is an upper bound, ask which known inequalit=
y would imply it. Working backward supplies a strategy; the finished proof =
must still connect the actual hypotheses to the target in a valid direction=
.</p>
