<p><a id=3D"chapter-03-section-13"></a></p>
<h3>Writing for a reader</h3>
<p>Proofs can be concise after their dependencies are clear, but a beginner=
 benefits from explicit transitions. Introduce a witness before using it, e=
xplain why it belongs to the required domain, and mark the point where the =
goal has been obtained. A reader should not need to guess whether a symbol =
was chosen freely or solved from an equation. These are mathematical accura=
cy issues as well as writing issues: ambiguous dependency can turn a valid =
idea into an invalid argument.</p>
<p>An accepted theorem can be used as a lemma later without reproducing its=
 whole proof. State the theorem and check its hypotheses on the current obj=
ects. This keeps arguments readable while preserving their logical dependen=
cies. Reusing a theorem outside its domain, such as applying a finite-set p=
igeonhole conclusion to an infinite self-map, is a hypothesis error even if=
 the theorem itself is perfectly correct.</p>
