 style=3D"height:0.6889em;"></span><span class=3D"mord mathbb">N</span></sp=
an></span></span> has a least element. This is the well-ordering principle.=
 It does not say every subset has a least element: the empty subset has non=
e. Nor is the same statement true for all integers, since the negative inte=
gers have no least member. The positive real numbers have no least member b=
ecause half of any positive real is smaller and still positive.</p>
<p>A typical proof assumes some natural-number input fails, collects all fa=
iling inputs into a nonempty set, chooses its least member, then uses its m=
inimality to derive a contradiction. The phrase =E2=80=9Cleast counterexamp=
le=E2=80=9D is doing work: every smaller input in the domain must succeed.<=
/p>
