<p><a id=3D"chapter-04-section-8"></a></p>
<h3>Choosing a method</h3>
<p>Use direct induction when the next case extends the previous case, as in=
 a sum. Use strong induction when an object splits into several smaller obj=
ects, as in factorization or divide-and-conquer. Use well ordering when a h=
ypothetical least obstruction naturally yields a smaller obstruction. All t=
hree express the same absence of an infinite descent through natural-number=
 indices.</p>
<p>Well ordering is not limited to the standard ordering on naturals, but g=
eneralizations need proof that the chosen order is well founded. Lexicograp=
hic order on pairs of naturals, with the first coordinate compared first, h=
as no infinite descending chain; a process may decrease the first coordinat=
e while resetting the second. In contrast, arbitrary lexicographic orders o=
ver all integers allow endless descent. We will use natural-valued measures=
 in program termination before considering more general orders.</p>
