<p>The term =E2=80=9Cinfinite descent=E2=80=9D describes the contradiction =
that any solution would generate a smaller solution indefinitely. The proof=
 does not actually perform infinitely many reductions. Well ordering says a=
 nonempty solution set would already contain a least denominator, and one r=
eduction contradicts that least choice. This is another example of a finite=
 argument controlling an infinite domain.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-04-section-14"></a></p>
<h3>Choosing the base honestly</h3>
<p>For the claim that every integer amount at least eighteen is a combinati=
on of four and seven, checking eighteen is not enough for a step that subtr=
acts four. The next three amounts have no path back to eighteen using that =
step. Listing nineteen, twenty, and twenty-one closes those entry gaps. Thi=
s is not an optional stylistic improvement; it supplies cases the propagati=
on argument otherwise never reaches.</p>
<p>Likewise, an induction on even indices establishes only even-indexed cas=
es unless a separate argument covers odd indices. The phrase =E2=80=9Cfor a=
ll <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http:/=
/www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation=
