al">n</span></span></span></span>=E2=80=9D at the end cannot enlarge what t=
he base and step actually reach. Draw arrows between indices if a proof use=
s an unusual increment or multiple dependencies. The resulting dependency p=
icture often reveals a missing base case before any algebra is attempted.</=
p>
<p>The index is part of the theorem. Before finishing, check that the concl=
usion covers exactly the claimed starting point, increment, and domain, inc=
luding any exceptional initial cases.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-04-section-15"></a></p>
<h3>Laboratory and practice</h3>
<p>The recurrence lab computes initial terms and compares a recursive defin=
ition with a closed formula. Agreement helps catch a proposed formula's err=
ors. The induction argument above explains why agreement persists at every =
index. A lab with a finite input bound cannot establish well ordering or a =
universal theorem by enumeration alone.</p>
