<p><a id=3D"chapter-05-section-12"></a></p>
<h3>An empty-domain audit</h3>
<p>There is exactly one function from the empty domain to any codomain: it =
has no input-output pairs. It satisfies the function rule because there is =
no input lacking an output. This function is injective, since no two distin=
ct inputs collide. It is surjective only when the codomain is also empty, b=
ecause a nonempty codomain contains an unreached element. A function from a=
 nonempty domain into the empty set cannot exist.</p>
<p>These cases are not artificial puzzles. They explain why combinatorial c=
onventions work at zero size and why an implication may hold vacuously. Whe=
never a theorem claims that an injection or surjection exists, checking emp=
ty sets can reveal a missing nonemptiness condition. The familiar expressio=
n for the number of functions must be interpreted with the empty-function c=
onvention when both sizes are zero.</p>
<p>A relation on the empty set is reflexive, symmetric, antisymmetric, and =
transitive because each property universally quantifies over nonexistent in=
puts. It is also irreflexive. On a nonempty set, reflexivity and irreflexiv=
ity cannot both hold. This shows that the compatibility of relation propert=
ies can depend on the domain's size, another reason to keep domains explici=
t rather than treating adjectives as isolated labels.</p>
<p>For a new relation, test the definitions directly on a pair and a triple=
 before assigning names to it. A small example often reveals whether symmet=
ry, antisymmetry, and transitivity have been confused. It does not prove a =
universal property on an infinite domain, but it can expose a false claim i=
mmediately.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-05-section-13"></a></p>
<h3>Applying the distinctions</h3>
<p>A list of user IDs may contain duplicates; a set of IDs does not. Conver=
ting a list to a set can lose multiplicity and order. A table mapping each =
ID to exactly one balance is a function; a table of friendships is a relati=
on. A program specification should say which structure is intended before a=
 proof or count begins.</p>
<p>In the counting lab, compare ordered selections with unordered subsets. =
In the graph lab, the adjacency matrix represents a relation; a single row =
may contain several ones, so it is generally not a function. These experime=
nts reinforce the definitions rather than replace them.</p>
