<p><a id=3D"chapter-06"></a></p>
<h2>06. State machines, invariants, and recursive objects</h2>
<p>A computation changes over time. A proof about its final answer must exp=
lain what remains true as intermediate states change, and why a final state=
 is ever reached. A recursively built object poses a related question: what=
 remains true each time a constructor adds another piece? Source Chapters 6=
 and 7 connect these ideas through induction.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-06-section-1"></a></p>
<h3>States and transitions</h3>
<p>A state records all information relevant to the modeled process. A trans=
ition is an allowed change from one state to another. Initial states specif=
y where runs may begin. A run is a finite or infinite sequence in which eac=
h consecutive pair is an allowed transition. A reachable state occurs after=
 finitely many transitions from an initial state.</p>
