em;"></span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">m</span></span></span></span> p=
reserves a congruence invariant instead of an equality. This gives the effi=
cient exponentiation used in Chapter 08. The reasoning is structurally the =
same, but the algebraic environment changes. The proof must use valid modul=
ar multiplication laws rather than silently dividing residues.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-06-section-10"></a></p>
<h3>Safety, progress, and reachability</h3>
<p>A safety property says something bad never occurs, such as =E2=80=9Cthe =
balance never becomes negative.=E2=80=9D An invariant can prove safety if t=
he initial conditions and every transition preserve it. A progress property=
 says something good eventually occurs, such as =E2=80=9Ca waiting request =
receives service.=E2=80=9D Invariants alone usually cannot establish progre=
ss: a machine may remain forever in a safe state while doing nothing.</p>
<p>Termination is one progress property, established by a ranking function.=
 For nondeterministic or concurrent systems, a transition system can includ=
e multiple possible next moves. To claim every run progresses, assumptions =
about which moves are eventually selected may be necessary. A scheduler tha=
t is allowed to ignore a request forever defeats an eventual-service claim =
even if a servicing transition is always available. We do not assume fairne=
ss of scheduling unless stated.</p>
<p>Reachability is also stronger than satisfying a preserved equation. In a=
 machine that adds two to a counter starting at zero, every reachable value=
 is nonnegative and even. The invariant =E2=80=9Cnonnegative=E2=80=9D is tr=
ue but includes unreachable odd values. The stronger parity invariant rules=
 those out. Selecting a stronger invariant is often how one proves an impos=
sibility that a weaker invariant cannot detect.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-06-section-11"></a></p>
<h3>A bracket-processing machine</h3>
<p>To test a parenthesis string, scan left to right while maintaining balan=
ce. An opening adds one and a closing subtracts one. Reject immediately if =
balance becomes negative; accept at the end exactly when it is zero. The lo=
op invariant says the counter equals openings minus closings in the consume=
d prefix and no consumed prefix has negative balance. Each transition prese=
rves the counter relationship, while the rejection rule enforces the prefix=
 condition.</p>
<p>The rank is the number of unread symbols, which decreases by one. At ter=
mination, the characterization proved earlier tells us the acceptance condi=
tion is equivalent to membership in the balanced language. This is a comple=
te correctness argument combining a state invariant, a termination measure,=
 and an independently established language theorem. Merely counting equal n=
umbers of symbols at the end would miss a closing parenthesis that appears =
too early.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-06-section-12"></a></p>
<h3>Functions on trees and their cost</h3>
<p>Evaluation of a binary expression visits each node once if subexpression=
 results are computed once and stored during recursion. With constant-cost =
arithmetic at each node, the time is proportional to node count. If numeric=
 values become huge, arithmetic may no longer be constant cost; if a tree a=
ctually shares repeated subexpressions as a graph, revisiting shared nodes =
can duplicate work. A proof of value correctness and a proof of runtime req=
uire different models, even when they describe the same program.</p>
<p>Structural induction can prove both kinds of claim, but each uses its ow=
n predicate. For correctness, the predicate compares returned value with ma=
thematical meaning. For cost, it relates operation count to size. Keeping t=
hem separate helps avoid the mistake of treating a correct algorithm as aut=
omatically efficient or an efficient-looking routine as automatically corre=
ct.</p>
<p>When testing an implementation against an invariant, record the full sta=
te before and after every transition. A final correct answer can conceal a =
broken intermediate relationship that happened to repair itself on one inpu=
t. Conversely, a failed invariant check may reflect a mistaken proposed inv=
ariant rather than a faulty program. The preservation proof helps distingui=
sh those possibilities by stating exactly what the transition is supposed t=
o preserve.</p>
<p>For recursive objects, record the construction tree when a property seem=
s to fail. The relevant induction hypotheses apply to immediate component o=
bjects, not arbitrary substrings or unrelated earlier examples. Identifying=
 the constructor at the root often makes both the proof obligation and a pa=
rsing error clear.</p>
