<p><a id=3D"chapter-07-section-2"></a></p>
<h3>Countable unions and finite descriptions</h3>
<p>A countable union of countable sets is countable when enumerations of th=
e component sets are available. Arrange their lists in rows and traverse di=
agonally. For finite strings over a finite alphabet, group by length. Every=
 length has finitely many strings, so listing length zero, then one, then t=
wo, and so on enumerates them all.</p>
<p>Programs written in a fixed finite character alphabet are finite strings=
, hence form a countable set. The subset of syntactically valid programs is=
 still countable. This remains true even when programs may be arbitrarily l=
ong. =E2=80=9CNo fixed maximum length=E2=80=9D differs from allowing an act=
ually infinite program description.</p>
<p>There are countably many integer polynomials because each is described b=
y a finite list of integer coefficients. Each nonzero polynomial has finite=
ly many complex roots, so algebraic numbers form a countable set. We need n=
ot use this consequence later, but it illustrates how finite descriptions c=
onstrain the possible collection of objects.</p>
