"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;"><=
/span><span class=3D"mopen">{</span><span class=3D"mord">0</span><span clas=
s=3D"mpunct">,</span><span class=3D"mspace" style=3D"margin-right:0.1667em;=
"></span><span class=3D"mord">1</span><span class=3D"mclose">}</span></span=
></span></span> correspond to infinite bit sequences, so there are uncounta=
bly many. Programs are countable. Each program computes at most one such to=
tal function, so some functions are not computable by any program. This is =
an existence argument independent of the halting proof.</p>
<p>Countability alone does not give an algorithm listing all total computab=
le functions with a usable evaluation procedure. Syntactically listing prog=
rams includes programs that fail to halt. Distinguish listing descriptions =
from effectively identifying the descriptions with a semantic property. Man=
y subtle errors in computability come from treating those tasks as intercha=
ngeable.</p>
<p>The collection of languages over a finite alphabet is also uncountable: =
a language is any subset of the countable set of finite strings. There are =
therefore more languages than programs that decide them. This connects abst=
ract cardinality directly to limits on computational recognition and decisi=
on.</p>
