out a bounding set. That distinction motivates restricted comprehension: fr=
om an existing set, select members satisfying a property. There is no set o=
f all sets in the standard framework.</p>
<p>Sets are foundational objects, not necessarily physical containers. Cont=
ainer analogies help with membership but fail if they encourage a universal=
 container holding every possible set. A formal theory needs axioms determi=
ning which collections count as sets and which operations produce new ones.=
</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-07-section-8"></a></p>
<h3>What ZFC supplies</h3>
<p>Zermelo=E2=80=93Fraenkel set theory with choice, ZFC, is a common founda=
tion. Extensionality says sets with the same members are equal. Empty-set a=
nd pairing principles provide basic small sets. Union collects members from=
 the sets in a given set. Power set supplies all subsets of a given set. In=
finity supplies an infinite set from which natural numbers can be construct=
ed.</p>
<p>Separation permits selecting members of an existing set by a property, a=
voiding unrestricted comprehension. Replacement says images of a set under =
a definable functional rule form a set, supporting larger constructions. Fo=
undation excludes certain membership cycles and infinite descending members=
hip patterns. Choice says a family of nonempty sets admits a function selec=
ting one member from each; unlike finite explicit selections, an arbitrary =
family may lack a displayed selection rule.</p>
<p>Some axiom lists derive the empty-set axiom from another axiom instead o=
f listing it separately. We are explaining their roles, not presenting a co=
mplete formal language or deriving arithmetic line by line. The source like=
wise uses familiar mathematical facts for ordinary proofs. Foundations expl=
ain the legitimacy and limitations of the tools; they need not make every p=
ractical proof unreadably primitive.</p>
<p>G=C3=B6del's incompleteness results, in their applicable form, say suffi=
ciently expressive consistent effectively axiomatized theories cannot prove=
 every arithmetic truth, and cannot establish their own consistency under t=
he usual formal assumptions. This is a statement with technical hypotheses,=
 not a reason to abandon proof or treat all claims as equally uncertain. We=
 cite it as a foundational limit rather than attempting its advanced proof =
here.</p>
