<p><a id=3D"chapter-07-section-11"></a></p>
<h3>Deciding, recognizing, and timing out</h3>
<p>A decider must halt on every input with the correct yes-or-no answer. A =
recognizer for a language must halt and accept members, but may run forever=
 on nonmembers. Running a program for a million steps and reporting that it=
 has not halted is a correct observation about that finite interval. Report=
ing that it will never halt would add an unsupported universal claim about =
the future execution.</p>
<p>Some programs' termination is straightforward: a natural-number rank dec=
reases. Some loops are proved nonterminating by an invariant and a transiti=
on that always remains enabled. The halting theorem does not invalidate eit=
her kind of proof. It rules out one algorithm that always decides all such =
cases. A practical analysis tool may deliberately sacrifice completeness, a=
sk for annotations, restrict its input language, or return =E2=80=9Cunknown=
.=E2=80=9D These choices avoid the impossible specification while retaining=
 useful guarantees.</p>
<p>An uncomputable problem also differs from a computable but expensive pro=
blem. SAT for a finite formula is decidable by exhaustive assignment checki=
ng. Whether a much faster general method exists is a complexity question. U=
niversal halting is not merely a case where exhaustive search is too slow; =
a search for a halting event has no finite stopping certificate in every no=
nhalting case. Separating decidability from efficiency is a central CS dist=
inction.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-07-section-12"></a></p>
<h3>What diagonalization does and does not construct</h3>
<p>In Cantor's proof, the new subset is explicitly defined relative to the =
alleged listing. This does not require an algorithm that computes membershi=
p in every listed set. The proof is set-theoretic: it reasons about arbitra=
ry functions and memberships. In the halting proof, by contrast, the assume=
d decider is computational, so the constructed disagreeing program must its=
elf be a legitimate finite program. The diagonal pattern is shared, while t=
he surrounding framework differs.</p>
<p>Similarly, the statement that uncomputable functions exist does not supp=
ly a finite table of all their values. A function may be mathematically wel=
l defined without being computable. =E2=80=9CDefined,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Cdes=
cribed,=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9Ccomputed=E2=80=9D have related but distinct m=
eanings. Later mathematical models often use ideal objects even when only a=
pproximations are computationally available.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-07-section-13"></a></p>
<h3>Consistency and ordinary mathematical work</h3>
<p>Axioms are not conclusions obtained from experiments. They specify a for=
mal framework within which implications can be proved. A contradiction in t=
hat framework would be serious because classical logic would then allow eve=
ry statement to be derived. Carefully restricting set formation is therefor=
e part of making mathematics coherent, not an arbitrary inconvenience impos=
ed on an otherwise harmless universal set.</p>
<p>At the same time, a beginner need not formalize every arithmetic operati=
on in set theory before proving a sum identity. We work at a useful level o=
f abstraction, openly accepting familiar number laws. Foundations explain w=
hat a full formal account would need and where its limits lie. This balance=
 allows rigor without burying each useful idea under thousands of primitive=
 derivation steps.</p>
<p>Countability also differs from having a physically writable completed li=
st. An enumeration is a rule assigning a finite position to each element; n=
o person finishes the infinite task. Uncountability says even such an ideal=
 assignment of positions cannot exist. This distinction prevents an unhelpf=
ul objection that no infinite set can be listed because life is finite. The=
 mathematical definitions concern functions and exhaustive coverage, not av=
ailable handwriting time or computer storage.</p>
<p>A finite prefix of an enumeration illustrates the rule; its proof must e=
stablish that every permitted object eventually occurs.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-07-section-14"></a></p>
<h3>Practice and perspective</h3>
<p>No finite simulation demonstrates an uncountable set or solves universal=
 halting. The bracket and recursion experiments from Chapter 06 are useful =
examples of restricted systems where termination and membership are decidab=
le. Knowing why these restricted algorithms work helps locate what the univ=
ersal impossibility result actually excludes.</p>
