<p>Euclid is efficient because the remainder decreases rapidly, not just be=
cause it decreases eventually. Over two divisions, the relevant positive va=
lue falls by at least a factor of two: if a remainder is more than half its=
 predecessor, the next remainder is their difference and is less than half.=
 Thus the number of divisions is logarithmic in the input magnitude. The co=
st of each division also depends on the number of input bits; counting divi=
sions is a particular cost model.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-08-section-3"></a></p>
<h3>Primes and unique factorization</h3>
