<p><a id=3D"chapter-08-section-13"></a></p>
<h3>From decision to search</h3>
<p>The SAT factoring connection illustrates a reusable reduction pattern. A=
 decision oracle tells whether a completion exists, not necessarily which c=
ompletion. To recover a witness, ask whether one proposed first bit can be =
extended to a valid solution. Keep it if yes, otherwise choose the other bi=
t, assuming the current partial assignment was known to be extendable. Repe=
at for each remaining bit. Each answer preserves the invariant that at leas=
t one valid completion remains.</p>
<p>At the end, the complete assignment is a witness because it satisfies al=
l constraints. The number of oracle calls is linear in the witness length. =
This is a proof about hypothetical access to a correct solver and the size =
of the encoding; it does not claim that the solver itself is fast. Separati=
ng the reduction's cost from the oracle's cost prevents circular conclusion=
s about efficiency.</p>
<p>A useful final audit for any congruence calculation is to substitute the=
 proposed residues into the original statement and compare remainders in th=
e declared range. This independently catches errors from negative represent=
atives, a changed modulus, or an inverse computed for the wrong number. Ver=
ification of selected answers does not replace the theorem proving that all=
 solutions have been found; completeness comes from the gcd criterion and t=
he reduced congruence.</p>
