<h3>Walks, paths, and cycles</h3>
<p>A walk is a sequence of vertices with an edge from each entry to the nex=
t. Vertices may repeat. A path has no repeated vertices. A directed cycle r=
eturns to its starting vertex with otherwise distinct vertices. Length coun=
ts edges, so <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=
=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo sepa=
rator=3D"true">,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo separator=3D"true">,</mo><mi>d</mi></mro=
w><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">a,b,d</annotation></semantics>=
</math></span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=
=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.=
1944em;"></span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">a</span><span class=3D"mpun=
ct">,</span><span class=3D"mspace" style=3D"margin-right:0.1667em;"></span>=
<span class=3D"mord mathnormal">b</span><span class=3D"mpunct">,</span><spa=
n class=3D"mspace" style=3D"margin-right:0.1667em;"></span><span class=3D"m=
ord mathnormal">d</span></span></span></span> has length two. A length-zero=
 walk stays at its single vertex.</p>
<p>If a walk from <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math =
xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi></m=
row><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></=
math></span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"=
base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height:0.4306em;"></span><span class=
=3D"mord mathnormal">u</span></span></span></span> to <span class=3D"katex"=
><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/Ma=
thML"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application=
/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=3D"katex-html" =
aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"he=
ight:0.4306em;"></span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal" style=3D"margin-righ=
t:0.03588em;">v</span></span></span></span> repeats a vertex, remove the se=
gment between two appearances. The remaining sequence is still a walk with =
the same endpoints and fewer edges. Repeating this removal gives a path. Th=
us reachability by a walk implies reachability by a path, and a shortest wa=
lk never repeats a vertex except in a separate cycle question.</p>
<p>This gives a finite reachability bound: in a graph with <span class=3D"k=
atex"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Ma=
