<p><a id=3D"chapter-09-section-4"></a></p>
<h3>Directed acyclic graphs</h3>
<p>A DAG is a directed graph with no directed cycle. Every nonempty finite =
DAG has a vertex of indegree zero. Otherwise repeatedly follow an incoming =
edge backward; finiteness forces a repeated vertex, creating a cycle. Finit=
eness is used exactly at that repetition step.</p>
<p>A topological ordering lists all vertices so every edge points from an e=
arlier to a later entry. Repeatedly remove an indegree-zero vertex and outp=
ut it. The remaining graph is still a DAG, so the process can continue unti=
l empty. This proves existence and supplies an algorithm. If the process ge=
ts stuck before all vertices are output, the remaining graph contains a cyc=
le.</p>
