<h3>Parallel schedules, chains, and antichains</h3>
<p>A chain is a set whose elements are pairwise comparable. An antichain is=
 a set with no two distinct comparable elements. In a dependency DAG, tasks=
 in a chain must run sequentially; tasks in an antichain have no precedence=
 relation between them. Resource conflicts may still prevent simultaneous e=
xecution, so precedence is only one part of a real scheduling model.</p>
<p>Assume each task takes one time unit and processors are unlimited. Defin=
e depth of a vertex as the maximum number of edges in a path ending there. =
Schedule all depth-zero tasks first, depth-one tasks next, and so on. Every=
 edge increases depth by at least one, so prerequisites finish earlier. If =
a longest path contains <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-mathml">=
<math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</=
mi></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">h</annotation></semant=
ics></math></span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span cla=
ss=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height:0.6944em;"></span><span c=
lass=3D"mord mathnormal">h</span></span></span></span> vertices, this sched=
ule takes <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D=
