<p><a id=3D"chapter-09-section-12"></a></p>
<h3>Finite scheduling with a processor limit</h3>
<p>The unlimited-processor critical-path formula gives a lower bound when p=
rocessors are limited, but another lower bound is total work divided by pro=
cessor count, rounded up for unit tasks. If ten independent unit tasks have=
 no edges and there are three processors, the longest chain has size one, y=
et at least four time slots are needed. Both lower bounds must be considere=
d.</p>
<p>A legal greedy schedule selects available tasks whose predecessors are f=
inished. It always respects dependencies, but choosing among available task=
s can affect total time under a processor limit. Finding a legal ordering a=
nd finding an optimal resource-constrained schedule are different problems.=
 Our laboratory computes the unlimited-processor layering and labels that a=
ssumption instead of silently presenting it as an optimizer for every pract=
ical project.</p>
