<p>The product order on pairs offers a useful interpretation in optimizatio=
n: one pair can dominate another coordinatewise while some pairs represent =
tradeoffs. A lexicographic order resolves every tradeoff by prioritizing th=
e first coordinate absolutely. Neither choice is universally correct; the i=
ntended meaning determines which order represents the problem. Mathematics =
makes that choice explicit and then reasons from it.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-09-section-14"></a></p>
<h3>Equivalence classes as replacement objects</h3>
<p>When grouping objects by an equivalence relation, one can work with the =
set of classes, called a quotient set. A function defined on classes must b=
e independent of the representative chosen. Modular addition passes this te=
st because congruent inputs have congruent sums. A rule such as =E2=80=9Cta=
ke the ordinary integer value of a residue class=E2=80=9D fails because a c=
lass contains many integers.</p>
<p>This representative-independence requirement is a precise proof obligati=
on. Before using a quotient construction, choose two representatives of the=
 same class and check that the proposed outputs agree in the required sense=
. It is the same general habit of making hidden assumptions visible that we=
 used in inverse functions and modular cancellation.</p>
<p>When a graph drawing and an adjacency matrix appear to disagree, compare=
 the vertex order first. A permutation of labels simultaneously permutes ro=
ws and columns. The resulting matrix can look different while representing =
an isomorphic graph. Numerical entries should therefore be interpreted with=
 the displayed vertex labels, never as unlabeled positions alone.</p>
