<p><a id=3D"chapter-10-section-2"></a></p>
<h3>Isomorphism</h3>
<p>Two graphs are isomorphic if a vertex bijection preserves adjacency in b=
oth directions. Labels and drawing positions do not matter. Equal vertex co=
unts, edge counts, and degree multisets are necessary conditions, not suffi=
cient ones. A six-cycle and two disjoint triangles both have six degree-two=
 vertices, but one is connected and the other is not, so they are not isomo=
rphic.</p>
<p>To prove isomorphism, exhibit the bijection and check all adjacency. To =
disprove it, find an invariant such as component count, triangle count, or =
degree pattern that differs. Failure to find a pleasing drawing is not a pr=
oof of nonisomorphism.</p>
