<p>Kruskal's algorithm considers edges in nondecreasing weight order and ac=
cepts an edge exactly when it connects different current components. The cu=
t property justifies each safe addition. Prim's algorithm grows one tree by=
 selecting the lightest outgoing edge from its vertex set. Tied weights can=
 yield multiple MSTs, so uniqueness requires extra argument.</p>
<p>For a triangle with edge weights one, two, and four, an MST uses one and=
 two, total three. Choosing the two smallest works here; the general algori=
thm must also avoid cycles. A four-vertex example may have its three smalle=
st edges all within one triangle, so taking the globally smallest <span cla=
