<p><a id=3D"chapter-10-section-7"></a></p>
<h3>Networks require measurement conventions</h3>
<p>A switching network has input terminals, output terminals, switches, and=
 directed connections. A permutation routing problem sends one packet from =
each input <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=
=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow><=
annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>=
</span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"=
><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height:0.6595em;"></span><span class=3D"mor=
d mathnormal">i</span></span></span></span> to distinct output <span class=
=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/19=
98/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>=CF=80</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">(</m=
o><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"app=
lication/x-tex">\pi(i)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=3D=
"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut=
" style=3D"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;"></span><span class=3D"mord m=
athnormal" style=3D"margin-right:0.03588em;">=CF=80</span><span class=3D"mo=
pen">(</span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">i</span><span class=3D"mclose"=
>)</span></span></span></span>. A solution chooses one route for each packe=
t.</p>
<p>Diameter is the maximum, over input/output pairs, of the length of a sho=
rtest route. The congestion of chosen routes is the maximum number of their=
 packets that visit one switch. The network's worst-case permutation conges=
tion takes, for each permutation, the best achievable congestion, then the =
worst permutation. This order of minimization and maximization matters.</p>
<p>Latency in the source's static routing discussion concerns route lengths=
; real time additionally depends on buffering and a packet schedule. Low di=
ameter does not imply low congestion: many short routes may collide at one =
central switch. Switch count and switch port size measure cost differently,=
 so count both.</p>
