<p><a id=3D"chapter-10-section-10"></a></p>
<h3>Separating several kinds of graph question</h3>
<p>A graph can have a spanning tree even when it has many cycles; the spann=
ing tree uses only a selected subset of edges. A minimum spanning tree mini=
mizes total selected weight, while a shortest path minimizes one route. A m=
atching selects disjoint edges rather than connecting every vertex into one=
 component. A coloring labels vertices rather than selecting edges. These d=
ifferent output types are a useful first check when choosing a theorem for =
an application.</p>
<p>For example, assigning three workers to three jobs asks for a matching, =
not a spanning tree of the worker=E2=80=93job graph. Connecting four office=
s with minimum cable asks for an MST when arbitrary chosen links are allowe=
d and reliability requirements do not demand redundancy. Scheduling conflic=
ting exams asks for a vertex coloring. Misidentifying the output structure =
can lead to a mathematically correct solution of the wrong problem.</p>
