<p><a id=3D"chapter-10-section-12"></a></p>
<h3>Why a greedy MST exchange remains safe</h3>
<p>At a stage of Kruskal's algorithm, accepted edges form a forest. If a ne=
xt candidate joins two different components, consider the cut separating on=
e component from the rest. No earlier lighter edge crossing that cut was sa=
fely skipped: if it had connected components then, it would have been accep=
ted. The selected edge is therefore a light safe connector for the current =
structure. The exchange argument shows there is still an MST containing eve=
ry accepted edge.</p>
<p>The maintained claim is not =E2=80=9Cthe current forest is already a spa=
nning tree.=E2=80=9D It is =E2=80=9Csome optimum can extend the current for=
est.=E2=80=9D This is an invariant about the set of possible completions. O=
nce the forest spans and is connected, the completion is the forest itself,=
 so it is optimal. The pattern is related to the decision-to-search invaria=
nt in Chapter 08, though the mathematical objects differ.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-10-section-13"></a></p>
<h3>Numerical network comparison</h3>
<p>At eight inputs, the tree uses fifteen switches, the array sixty-four, t=
he butterfly thirty-two, and the source-convention Bene=C5=A1 forty-eight. =
Their diameters are eight, sixteen, five, and seven. Worst permutation cong=
estion is eight, two, two, and one. No single number tells the entire desig=
n story: a Bene=C5=A1 spends more switches than the butterfly to obtain rou=
te flexibility and eliminate static switch conflicts.</p>
<p>The table's congestion one means selected packet paths are switch-disjoi=
nt in the modeled permutation routing, not that every physical network impl=
ementation has zero delay under all traffic. Terminal conventions, link ban=
dwidth, switch timing, buffering, and repeated traffic rounds belong to add=
itional engineering models. Precisely stating what a theorem measures makes=
 it more useful, because its conclusion can be carried into a richer model =
without accidentally claiming more than was proved.</p>
