<p><a id=3D"chapter-11"></a></p>
<h2>11. Planarity, faces, and polyhedra</h2>
<p>A tangled graph drawing does not prove the graph inherently needs crossi=
ngs. Planarity asks whether some crossing-free drawing exists. Source Chapt=
er 13 develops a discrete account of embeddings, Euler's formula, edge boun=
ds, coloring, and regular polyhedra. We assume the graph vocabulary, degree=
 sum, and induction arguments already established.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-11-section-1"></a></p>
<h3>Graph versus embedding</h3>
<p>A graph is planar if it can be drawn in the plane with edges meeting onl=
y at shared endpoints. A particular crossing-free drawing is a plane embedd=
ing. Curving or rerouting edges can remove crossings without changing adjac=
ency. A drawing of a square with one diagonal is planar; a careless drawing=
 may introduce crossings that are avoidable.</p>
<p>An embedding divides the plane into faces, including the unbounded outer=
 face. A triangle has two faces: interior and exterior. A tree has only one=
 face, even if its drawn edges look like boundaries of narrow regions. Face=
s belong to an embedding, not to a list of vertex degrees alone.</p>
<p>The sphere offers an equivalent view. Project a crossing-free plane draw=
ing onto a sphere, leaving one point unused. Conversely, remove a point fro=
m a spherical face and project to the plane. The face containing the remove=
d point becomes the outer face. =E2=80=9COuter=E2=80=9D is therefore a feat=
ure of the chosen planar presentation rather than a mathematically privileg=
ed face.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-11-section-2"></a></p>
<h3>Discrete boundaries and recursive construction</h3>
<p>One can record an embedding by a cyclic order of incident edges around e=
ach vertex. Following an edge with a consistently chosen side traces a face=
 boundary. A bridge is encountered twice along the boundary of the same fac=
e, once on each side. Thus boundary length counts edge-side incidences, not=
 necessarily distinct edges or vertices.</p>
<p>For connected plane graphs, a recursive construction starts with a singl=
e vertex, adds an edge to a new vertex without creating a face, or adds an =
edge between existing vertices along a face to split that face. The new edg=
e's placement matters: an abstract edge between two vertices is not automat=
ically drawable within a particular face if they are not both incident with=
 it.</p>
<p>These operations explain the bookkeeping behind Euler's formula. A leaf =
addition increases both vertices and edges by one. A face-splitting edge in=
creases edges and faces by one. Both preserve <span class=3D"katex"><span c=
lass=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><s=
emantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=E2=88=92</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>f</mi></=
