<p>Both characterizations are stronger than edge-count tests. Their full pr=
oofs are outside our scope, as they are outside the source's introductory e=
xposition. To show nonplanarity in an example, exhibiting a forbidden minor=
 or subdivision is a valid certificate using the stated theorem. To show pl=
anarity, provide a crossing-free embedding; merely failing to spot a forbid=
den structure is not enough.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-11-section-8"></a></p>
<h3>Counting face boundaries with bridges</h3>
<p>Draw a triangle and attach one extra leaf to one triangle vertex. There =
are four vertices and four edges. The embedding has the triangle's interior=
 and a single exterior, so there are two faces. Euler gives four minus four=
 plus two equals two. The interior boundary length is three; the exterior b=
oundary follows the triangle's three sides and traverses the leaf edge out =
and back, giving length five. The total is eight, equal to twice the edge c=
ount.</p>
<p>This example shows why a face boundary need not be a simple cycle. Treat=
ing every boundary as a list of distinct edges would undercount bridges. It=
 also shows why the degree of a face in the counting proof means the number=
 of incident edge sides, including repeated incidences. A definition that l=
ooks fussy is protecting the theorem from a concrete edge case.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-11-section-9"></a></p>
<h3>Applying the disconnected formula</h3>
<p>Take a square cycle and a separate isolated vertex. The graph has five v=
ertices, four edges, two faces, and two components. Its Euler quantity is t=
hree, equal to one plus the component count. Add an edge from the isolated =
vertex to the square through the outer face. The component count falls to o=
ne, the edge count rises to five, and the face count remains two. The conne=
cted formula now gives two.</p>
<p>An isolated vertex does not divide a face into two. A bridge between com=
ponents also does not create a new face. Only an edge that closes an approp=
riate cycle in an embedding splits a region. These observations supply the =
geometric meaning behind the algebraic adjustments, so the formulas need no=
t be memorized as unrelated cases.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-11-section-10"></a></p>
<h3>Edge bounds can be tight</h3>
<p>A planar triangulation has every face bounded by three edge sides, so eq=
uality holds in the general edge bound. Starting from a triangle, place a n=
ew vertex inside a triangular face and connect it to its three corners. Thi=
s adds one vertex, three edges, and two net faces while preserving a triang=
ulation. At each stage the equality <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"ka=
