<p>An asymptotic upper bound can also be valid without being tight. Saying =
a linear function is big O of a cubic is correct under the definition, but =
may answer a performance question poorly. A proof of Theta supplies matchin=
g scale in both directions. A proof of little o says one function becomes n=
egligible relative to another, a stronger comparison than merely being smal=
ler for the first thousand inputs. The notation is a vocabulary of differen=
t claims, not interchangeable ways to say =E2=80=9Capproximately.=E2=80=9D<=
/p>
<p>When comparing experimental timings with an asymptotic model, remember t=
hat the input variable and the measured operation must match. Time versus n=
umber of digits differs from time versus the represented integer's magnitud=
e. This distinction will matter for primality, modular exponentiation, and =
recurrence analysis throughout computer science.</p>
<p>For a final sum check, compare the answer with the number of terms times=
 their minimum and maximum. Any exact value outside those bounds is impossi=
ble.</p>
