<p><a id=3D"chapter-13"></a></p>
<h2>13. Counting without overcounting</h2>
<p>Before calculating a probability, we must know what outcomes exist and w=
hether each has the same weight. Counting is therefore a modeling disciplin=
e: identify the objects, construct them without ambiguity, and account for =
duplicate descriptions. Source Chapter 15 develops bijections, product and =
division rules, permutations, subsets, repeated objects, pigeonholes, inclu=
sion=E2=80=93exclusion, and combinatorial proofs.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-13-section-1"></a></p>
<h3>Bijections and disjoint choices</h3>
<p>A bijection transfers a difficult count to an easier collection. Subsets=
 of an <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"ht=
