ction, recurrence, generating function, or inclusion=E2=80=93exclusion; cho=
ose the representation that makes the constraint simplest.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-13-section-14"></a></p>
<h3>Why unordered selection is not always a division problem</h3>
<p>Suppose a bag contains repeated labels and we record only the visible la=
bel pair selected. Different pairs of physical objects can produce the same=
 visible pair in unequal numbers. Dividing a count of physical pairs by two=
 for order does not remove differences in label multiplicity. First decide =
whether objects are individually distinguishable or identified by type. The=
n determine what equivalence relation turns one description into another.</=
p>
<p>This issue also appears in probabilistic sampling. Uniformly selecting p=
hysical objects does not necessarily induce a uniform distribution on visib=
le patterns. A type with many physical representatives receives more probab=
ility. Counting the number of patterns and treating them as equally likely =
silently changes the experiment. The next chapters repeatedly use this dist=
inction between elementary outcomes and summaries of outcomes.</p>
