<p><a id=3D"chapter-13-section-16"></a></p>
<h3>Auditing a counting solution</h3>
<p>Ask four questions before accepting a formula. What is one object being =
counted? Does every valid object arise from the construction? Can one objec=
t arise in more than one way? If so, is the multiplicity constant and corre=
ctly removed? A fifth useful check is whether the expression behaves sensib=
ly at boundary values, including zero selections and selecting everything.<=
/p>
<p>For example, a committee count should be one when the committee is empty=
 and one when it includes the whole population. A password count may be zer=
o if a no-repetition length exceeds the alphabet size. A formula with facto=
rials of negative integers is not a signal to invent those factorials; it i=
s a signal that the case conditions were omitted. State a piecewise result =
or a convention such as out-of-range binomial coefficients equal zero.</p>
