<p>This explains the boundary subtractions in generating-function recurrenc=
e solutions. They are not cosmetic adjustments made to force an answer; the=
y keep the index transformation within the stated algebraic objects. A care=
ful derivation writes the starting index of every sum before replacing it w=
ith a shifted generating function.</p>
<p>The polynomial viewpoint also separates finite information from an infin=
ite specification. Multiplying truncated series correctly determines produc=
t coefficients only through the truncation order when omitted higher coeffi=
cients cannot contribute to those low powers. It does not determine all lat=
er coefficients. The laboratory multiplies complete finite polynomials, so =
its full output is exact for those inputs. When using it to approximate inf=
inite generating functions, interpret only the coefficients justified by th=
e truncation. This is another place where an implementation limit should be=
 stated in mathematical terms rather than hidden behind a plausible-looking=
 list.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-14-section-19"></a></p>
