<h1>Probability and stochastic models</h1>
<p>We attach weights to outcomes, extract numerical variables, control devi=
ations, and use recurrences and graph structure to study random processes.<=
/p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-15"></a></p>
<h2>15. Probability spaces and conditional reasoning</h2>
<p>When a result is uncertain, the first task is to specify what can happen=
 and how outcomes are weighted. A plausible story is not enough. Different =
rules for revealing information can produce different probabilities even wh=
en the visible result looks the same. Source Chapters 17 and 18 develop dis=
crete probability, conditional probability, independence, Bayesian updating=
, and the interpretation of confidence.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-15-section-1"></a></p>
