<p><a id=3D"chapter-15-section-3"></a></p>
<h3>Monty Hall and the information mechanism</h3>
<p>Assume three doors, one uniformly placed prize, an initial contestant ch=
oice, and a host who knows the location, always opens a different losing do=
or, and always offers a switch. Staying wins exactly when the initial choic=
e was right, probability one third. Switching wins exactly when it was wron=
g, probability two thirds. The host's knowledge and mandatory behavior are =
hypotheses of the result.</p>
<p>If instead a person opens another door uniformly without knowing the pri=
ze and we condition on a losing reveal, the model changes. Fix the initial =
choice as door one. If the prize is behind door one, a losing reveal occurs=
 with probability one; if behind door two or three, it occurs with probabil=
ity one half. The weighted surviving probabilities are <span class=3D"katex=
"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/M=
athML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=3D"normal">/</mi><mn>3</m=
n><mo separator=3D"true">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=3D"normal">/</mi><=
mn>6</mn><mo separator=3D"true">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=3D"normal">=
/</mi><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">1/3,1/6,1=
/6</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hi=
dden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height:1e=
m;vertical-align:-0.25em;"></span><span class=3D"mord">1/3</span><span clas=
s=3D"mpunct">,</span><span class=3D"mspace" style=3D"margin-right:0.1667em;=
"></span><span class=3D"mord">1/6</span><span class=3D"mpunct">,</span><spa=
n class=3D"mspace" style=3D"margin-right:0.1667em;"></span><span class=3D"m=
ord">1/6</span></span></span></span>, totaling <span class=3D"katex"><span =
class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><=
semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=3D"normal">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow=
><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">2/3</annotation></semantics></m=
ath></span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"b=
ase"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;"></s=
pan><span class=3D"mord">2/3</span></span></span></span>, so initial correc=
tness conditional on a losing reveal is one half. The same visible =E2=80=
=9Ca losing door opened=E2=80=9D carries different information under differ=
ent host rules.</p>
<p>When a specific door is observed, the host's tie-breaking policy can als=
o matter. Complete outcomes should include both prize location and host act=
ion. Conditioning on a broad event and conditioning on a particular observe=
d action are different events. This is why declaring the mechanism before c=
ounting is essential.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-15-section-4"></a></p>
<h3>Nontransitive dice</h3>
