<p>Rolling twice and comparing total scores creates a new random experiment=
. A single-roll win probability cannot simply be squared to obtain the chan=
ce of a larger sum. The sum can win despite one losing roll, and ties can m=
atter. Enumerate ordered roll pairs or convolve score distributions. This c=
aptures the source's unusual-dice lesson with an independently specified ex=
ample.</p>
