<p><a id=3D"chapter-15-section-10"></a></p>
<h3>Probability, belief, and confidence</h3>
<p>A model probability is a mathematical consequence of assumptions. Confid=
ence in the assumptions is a separate issue. A fair independent coin has ne=
xt-head probability one half regardless of a long past run. If the fairness=
 itself is uncertain, observations can update beliefs about its bias, and t=
he predictive probability can change. These are different models, not confl=
icting probability rules.</p>
<p>A low-probability observation does not by itself prove a model false: ra=
re events occur. Compare how likely the observation is under competing mode=
ls, and account for selection among many possible =E2=80=9Csurprises.=E2=80=
=9D If a million tests each look for a rare pattern, the chance that some t=
est surprises us can be substantial even when each individual probability i=
s small.</p>
<p>Frequentist confidence statements concern repeated-sampling coverage of =
a procedure; Bayesian posterior probabilities concern an unknown parameter =
under a prior-and-likelihood model. Neither should be silently substituted =
for the other. Chapter 17 develops explicit sampling bounds without requiri=
ng an advanced statistics course.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-15-section-11"></a></p>
<h3>A complete conditional table</h3>
<p>Use the synthetic detector example to build a two-by-two probability tab=
le. The malicious-and-flagged cell is 0.009, malicious-and-unflagged is 0.0=
01, benign-and-flagged is 0.0495, and benign-and-unflagged is 0.9405. All c=
ells are nonnegative and sum to one. The flag column totals 0.0585, so divi=
ding its malicious cell by that total gives the posterior probability. The =
table keeps every denominator visible.</p>
<p>Now change prevalence to ten percent while keeping the conditional flag =
rates. The cells become 0.09, 0.01, 0.045, and 0.855. Among flagged message=
