<p><a id=3D"chapter-15-section-12"></a></p>
<h3>Independence can change after conditioning</h3>
<p>Two independent fair coin tosses are independent before additional infor=
mation. Condition on the event that at least one toss is a head. The surviv=
ing outcomes are HH, HT, and TH, each equally likely. The conditional chanc=
e the first is heads is two thirds, as is the chance the second is heads, b=
ut the conditional chance both are heads is one third, not four ninths. Thu=
s conditioning can create dependence.</p>
<p>If instead we condition on the first toss being heads, the second remain=
s fair because of the original independence. These two examples are compati=
ble: the conditioned events differ. A statement that variables are independ=
ent should specify the model or conditioning context. In Bayesian calculati=
ons, conditional independence under a hypothesis is not the same as uncondi=
tional independence after mixing hypotheses.</p>
