<p><a id=3D"chapter-15-section-14"></a></p>
<h3>Selection effects and surprising observations</h3>
<p>Imagine searching many strings for an unusual run. The probability of a =
run at one preselected position differs from the probability that some posi=
tion contains it. Define an event for each possible position and use inclus=
ion=E2=80=93exclusion, a union bound, or another suitable calculation. Choo=
sing the most striking position after inspecting the data does not preserve=
 the original single-position probability.</p>
<p>The same issue appears in a claim that an observed coincidence was extra=
ordinarily unlikely. Every particular long sequence of fair tosses has a ti=
ny probability, but some sequence must occur. A useful test specifies a fam=
ily of surprising outcomes before seeing the result, or accounts for the se=
lection process. Probability answers a question about an event; it does not=
 decide which event deserves attention after the fact.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-15-section-15"></a></p>
<h3>Reading a probability answer responsibly</h3>
<p>An exact fraction can be a perfectly correct answer to the wrong sample =
space. Before checking arithmetic, ask whether complete outcomes were repre=
sented, whether their weights sum to one, and whether the observed informat=
ion was conditioned on rather than ignored. Then ask whether the event matc=
hes the words =E2=80=9Cat least,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Cexactly,=E2=80=9D or =E2=
=80=9Cbefore.=E2=80=9D These distinctions change sets of outcomes and there=
fore change probabilities.</p>
<p>A simulation uses the same model assumptions encoded in its random gener=
ator. Agreement with an analytic formula checks their consistency but does =
not validate that model against the real world. Real-world validation needs=
 appropriate observations and a measurement design. In this course, synthet=
ic examples keep that boundary visible so that probability is learned as co=
nditional mathematical reasoning rather than as automatic certainty about a=
ny story that mentions randomness.</p>
<p>For a final sanity check, compute a complement or a total in a second wa=
y. A full conditional probability table must still sum to one after rescali=
ng to the conditioned event. If a result lies outside zero and one, revisit=
 event definitions and denominators before rounding. A small rounding error=
 and a wrong model require different repairs.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-15-section-16"></a></p>
<h3>Practice and laboratory</h3>
<p>The probability lab computes exact birthday and Bayes quantities from vi=
sible parameters. The Monty lab contrasts the informed host with a random o=
pener. Seeded trials are estimates alongside exact model values; they are n=
ot proofs that real participants obey the model.</p>
