<p>Expected cost is not the cost of a typical individual run unless further=
 distributional information supports that interpretation. In the coupon pro=
blem, late rare waits can pull the mean upward. In a bounded binomial probl=
em, concentration may make the mean more representative. The next chapter s=
upplies tools for stating that representativeness quantitatively instead of=
 relying on the word =E2=80=9Caverage.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p>When reporting a mean from simulation, state the number of trials and an=
y cap on duration or payout. A cap changes the random variable being averag=
ed. If a theoretical expectation is infinite, every finite simulated sample=
 still has a finite arithmetic mean, so finite output alone cannot refute t=
he theory. The appropriate evidence is the analytical tail behavior and a p=
recise account of the simulated truncation.</p>
<p>State whether a reported expectation counts trials, failures, visits, or=
 elapsed transitions. An unmentioned one-step difference can otherwise appe=
ar to contradict an entirely correct formula.</p>
