<p><a id=3D"chapter-17-section-12"></a></p>
<h3>A bound for a shifted variable</h3>
<p>Suppose a nonnegative task cost is always at least five and has mean sev=
en. Markov applied directly bounds the probability of cost at least fifteen=
 by seven fifteenths. Apply it instead to <span class=3D"katex"><span class=
=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><seman=
tics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=E2=88=92</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encodin=
g=3D"application/x-tex">X-5</annotation></semantics></math></span><span cla=
ss=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"=
strut" style=3D"height:0.7667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;"></span><span cla=
ss=3D"mord mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right:0.07847em;">X</span><span clas=
s=3D"mspace" style=3D"margin-right:0.2222em;"></span><span class=3D"mbin">=
=E2=88=92</span><span class=3D"mspace" style=3D"margin-right:0.2222em;"></s=
pan></span><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height:0.644=
4em;"></span><span class=3D"mord">5</span></span></span></span>, whose mean=
 is two, at threshold ten. The resulting upper bound is one fifth, much str=
onger. The lower endpoint supplied useful information that the direct appli=
cation ignored.</p>
<p>If the assumed lower bound were incorrect, the shifted variable could be=
 negative and the proof would fail. This is a reminder that sharpening a bo=
und is not merely algebraic cleverness: it requires a valid additional fact=
 about the distribution. If all we know is a mean, a supposedly better univ=
ersal tail bound may be disproved by a two-point distribution.</p>
