<p><a id=3D"chapter-17-section-15"></a></p>
<h3>Why pairwise independence is enough for one calculation</h3>
<p>The variance expansion contains only single-variable square terms and tw=
o-variable cross terms. Therefore pairwise independence eliminates every co=
variance appearing in that particular expansion. The exponential moment of =
a sum, however, contains a product involving the whole collection. Factorin=
g that product generally needs mutual independence. The needed assumption f=
ollows from the algebraic structure of the proof, not from a general rankin=
g in which one theorem is simply =E2=80=9Cmore probabilistic=E2=80=9D than =
another.</p>
<p>The XOR example makes this visible. Three pairwise independent fair bits=
 have a sum with the same mean and variance as three mutually independent f=
air bits, but not the same full distribution. Under the XOR construction, n=
ot all eight bit triples occur. A tail bound derived from the full independ=
ent-product moment cannot be imported solely because the first two moments =
agree.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-17-section-16"></a></p>
<h3>A simulation can miss a rare tail</h3>
<p>If an event has probability one in a million, a thousand simulated trial=
s are likely to observe zero occurrences. The resulting empirical frequency=
 zero does not prove the event impossible. Exact enumeration or a valid ana=
lytical bound may be more informative than increasing a small simulation sl=
ightly. Conversely, observing one rare event in a small run does not establ=
ish a reliable estimate of its true rate.</p>
<p>Seeded simulations are reproducible examples, not additional independent=
 evidence each time reset is clicked. Repeating the same seed reproduces th=
e same observations. To study sampling variation, change the seed or run a =
larger declared collection of trials. The lab exposes this distinction so r=
eproducibility does not accidentally become false confidence.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-17-section-17"></a></p>
<h3>From a single failure bound to a system claim</h3>
<p>Suppose each of fifty components has a modeled failure probability at mo=
st one thousandth during a specified interval. The union bound gives system=
 probability of at least one failure at most five percent, even if failures=
 are dependent. If the failures are independent and all probabilities are e=
xactly one thousandth, the exact probability is one minus <span class=3D"ka=
tex"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Mat=
h/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>0.999</mn><mn>50</mn></msup></mrow><an=
notation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">0.999^{50}</annotation></semantics>=
</math></span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=
=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height:0.8141em;"></span><span cla=
ss=3D"mord">0.99</span><span class=3D"mord"><span class=3D"mord">9</span><s=
pan class=3D"msupsub"><span class=3D"vlist-t"><span class=3D"vlist-r"><span=
 class=3D"vlist" style=3D"height:0.8141em;"><span style=3D"top:-3.063em;mar=
gin-right:0.05em;"><span class=3D"pstrut" style=3D"height:2.7em;"></span><s=
pan class=3D"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class=3D"mord mtight"><=
span class=3D"mord mtight">50</span></span></span></span></span></span></sp=
an></span></span></span></span></span>, slightly below five percent. The ex=
act product needs assumptions the union bound did not need.</p>
<p>This example connects the chapter's tools. A mean count of failures is t=
he sum of component probabilities by linearity. Markov applied to that coun=
t gives the union bound for at least one failure. Independence can then sha=
rpen the answer through a product. Several apparently different formulas ar=
e instances of the same underlying relationship between indicators, counts,=
 and events.</p>
<p>An inequality can remain valid while becoming numerically useless. Cheby=
shev with a threshold smaller than the standard deviation may produce an up=
per bound above one. Reporting the minimum of that expression and one prese=
rves validity and communicates the lack of useful information. A negative b=
ound, by contrast, signals a parameter or algebra error. The laboratory che=
cks these elementary constraints before presenting a result.</p>
<p>For every concentration theorem, keep three items together: the event be=
ing bounded, the distributional assumptions, and the numerical expression. =
Separating them invites applying an attractive formula to a different event=
 or a dependent process. A concise correct answer can include all three wit=
hout reproducing the theorem's whole proof.</p>
<p>An applicable but loose bound is useful evidence about the theorem's inf=
ormation requirements. It is not an arithmetic failure that every bound dif=
fers from the exact probability.</p>
