<p><a id=3D"chapter-18-section-6"></a></p>
<h3>=E2=80=9CQuit while ahead=E2=80=9D and stopping rules</h3>
<p>For a fair walk, stopping when one unit ahead eventually succeeds with p=
robability one if unlimited losses and time are allowed in the mathematical=
 model. But the stopping time has infinite mean and interim losses are unbo=
unded. An argument that every fixed step has zero expected gain does not au=
tomatically justify exchanging an unbounded stopping rule with expectation.=
</p>
<p>Optional-stopping theorems provide conditions under which expected value=
 is preserved, such as bounded stopping times or other suitable integrabili=
ty bounds. We do not assume those hypotheses merely because a strategy soun=
ds sensible. The lesson is about the validity of an interchange, not a clai=
m that a clever stopping rule creates a practical guaranteed profit.</p>
