<p>With a dangling vertex, leaving its transition row all zeros violates th=
e row-sum condition and loses probability mass whenever a walker reaches it=
. Renormalizing afterward hides the modeling error by conditioning on survi=
val rather than repairing the intended process. Replacing the row with a de=
clared restart distribution supplies an actual transition rule and preserve=
s the original meaning of total probability.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-18-section-13"></a></p>
<h3>Detailed balance and its limits</h3>
<p>For an undirected graph walk, equal edge flow in both directions is call=
ed detailed balance. It implies stationarity by summing flows, but stationa=
rity need not imply detailed balance for a directed chain. Consider a direc=
ted three-cycle that moves deterministically forward. The uniform distribut=
ion is stationary, yet flow along each forward edge has no reverse counterp=
art. The chain circulates probability around the cycle.</p>
<p>This distinction helps interpret a ranking. A stationary distribution sa=
ys incoming mass balances outgoing mass at every vertex overall, not that e=
very pairwise exchange is balanced. Undirected degree formulas do not autom=
atically apply to directed graphs. There, solve the actual transition equat=
ions or use an appropriate theorem for the stated model.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-18-section-14"></a></p>
<h3>Why damping creates a contraction</h3>
<p>Take two row distributions <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-ma=
