erely because the graph looks well connected. It also explains why a dampin=
g parameter closer to one can require more iterations for a given tolerance=
. The result concerns this positive-teleport finite model; it does not asse=
rt a universal iteration count for every ranking implementation.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-18-section-15"></a></p>
<h3>Interpreting censored trials</h3>
<p>A simulation may stop each trial after a fixed step cap to keep the inte=
rface responsive. If a walk has not reached a boundary by that cap, the res=
ult is unknown for that trial. Counting it as ruin biases success downward;=
 counting it as success biases upward; discarding it can also bias an avera=
ge duration. Report the number censored and describe any estimate as condit=
ional on completed trials when that is what was calculated.</p>
<p>For small finite intervals, the cap can be large enough that censoring i=
s rare, but rarity should be observed and reported rather than assumed. The=
 exact formulas remain the reference values. A disagreement can indicate sa=
mpling fluctuation, censoring, a parameter mismatch, or an implementation e=
rror. The interface presents these quantities together to make those explan=
ations distinguishable.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-18-section-16"></a></p>
<h3>Connecting the final chapter to the first</h3>
<p>The fair-walk probability uses a linear recurrence, while its duration u=
ses a quadratic solution. The stationary model uses a function on probabili=
ty vectors and an invariant that total mass is one. The convergence argumen=
t uses a geometric decrease. Each idea was developed earlier in a simpler s=
etting. This cumulative structure is why the course begins with equations, =
functions, and proof instead of presenting random-walk formulas as isolated=
 facts.</p>
<p>When facing a new stochastic process, repeat the same sequence: define s=
tates, specify transitions, identify the target event or cost, condition on=
 the first step, state boundary conditions, solve, and verify a small insta=
nce. This method is more transferable than memorizing a single gambler's-ru=
in expression.</p>
<p>The stationary vector is normalized because multiplying any stationary s=
olution by a constant still satisfies the homogeneous balance equations. Wi=
thout a total-mass condition, the all-zero vector would also qualify algebr=
aically but would not be a probability distribution. Check nonnegativity an=
d sum one as well as the balance equations. This is another example where s=
olving equations is only part of verifying that the solution represents the=
 intended mathematical object.</p>
<p>For a reducible finite chain, several closed classes can support differe=
nt stationary distributions, and mixtures can also be stationary. A unique =
numerical result from one initial distribution does not prove uniqueness ov=
er all possible initializations. The positive teleportation model avoids th=
is ambiguity by making every state communicate with every other in one step=
.</p>
<p><a id=3D"chapter-18-section-17"></a></p>
<h3>Practice and synthesis</h3>
<p>The random-walk lab displays exact finite-boundary success probability a=
nd expected duration beside a seeded simulation. Capped trials are reported=
 as censored rather than silently treated as losses. The ranking lab comput=
es stationary probabilities from a visible small graph and displays the res=
idual; damping and iteration controls show their actual effect.</p>
